WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.859 --> 00:00:09.590
In our previous video, we studied different
parts of the human eye and their

00:00:09.590 --> 00:00:10.590
functions.

00:00:10.590 --> 00:00:13.980
In this video we are going to roughly see
how light propagates

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:18.710
inside the eye and how we are able to see
the objects around us.

00:00:18.710 --> 00:00:24.380
And for that, we will consider a simplified
2 dimensional image of our eye!

00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:26.290
So how does light travel inside?

00:00:26.290 --> 00:00:30.960
Suppose we are looking at an object which
is some distance away…

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:33.090
Not too far
away though.

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:36.960
The light from that object enters our eye
and falls on the

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:38.370
CORNEA first.

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:40.240
What is cornea?

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:45.080
It is this curved and transparent outer layer
guarding the entrance to the eye!

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:48.910
First, the light is REFRACTED by the cornea.

00:00:48.910 --> 00:00:53.370
This refracted light then travels
through the transparent fluid present in between

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:56.640
which is also called the
AQUEOUS HUMOR.

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:00.220
From this fluid, light then enters the pupil
and falls on the

00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:02.460
lens inside.

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:07.290
This eye lens is a biconvex lens and it refracts
the light such that the all light

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:11.880
coming from a point on an object is FOCUSED
on the retina.

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:13.540
This is where the
image is formed.

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:18.250
Clearly the image that you see is a real and
inverted image!

00:01:18.250 --> 00:01:22.150
But wait, we don’t really see the world
around us being upside down do we?

00:01:22.150 --> 00:01:28.720
We see everything as is because this image
again gets upright in the brain.

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:31.930
We
know that these light signals travel through

00:01:31.930 --> 00:01:35.799
the optic nerve as impulses and
travel to this part of the brain.

00:01:35.799 --> 00:01:39.650
It is known as OCCIPITAL LOBE.

00:01:39.650 --> 00:01:42.920
However before
this light falls on the retina, it first travels

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:46.650
in the space between the retina and
the lens.

00:01:46.650 --> 00:01:50.140
This space is filled up with another transparent
fluid called VITREOUS

00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:51.140
HUMOR.

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:55.060
Yes, there’s aqueous humor between the cornea
and the lens!

00:01:55.060 --> 00:01:57.540
And
there’s vitreous humor between the lens

00:01:57.540 --> 00:01:59.420
and the retina!

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:02.110
So this is how the light travels inside the
eye!

00:02:02.110 --> 00:02:05.640
There are multiple refractions
occurring inside!

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:08.849
In fact much of the refraction occurs before
the light reaches

00:02:08.849 --> 00:02:09.849
the lens!

00:02:09.849 --> 00:02:12.590
Let me get a bit technical here!

00:02:12.590 --> 00:02:15.480
Look at the eye closely.

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:17.650
What are the different mediums through which
light

00:02:17.650 --> 00:02:19.130
travels before it reaches the retina!

00:02:19.130 --> 00:02:22.260
There’s air; then there’s cornea; then
we

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:26.510
have the aqueous humor; then the lens; and
finally through the vitreous

00:02:26.510 --> 00:02:28.200
humor.

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:32.750
And here are the approximate refractive indices
of the media!

00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:37.660
Now if you notice, the refractive indices
of air and the cornea are largely

00:02:37.660 --> 00:02:38.780
different!

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:42.340
Whereas the others are not significantly different.

00:02:42.340 --> 00:02:47.930
Hence most of the
refraction happens are the air-cornea interface.

00:02:47.930 --> 00:02:53.530
Refraction does occur at other
interfaces too, but not as much as this interface!

00:02:53.530 --> 00:02:58.129
The lens also refracts the light; however
its main function is to make sure all

00:02:58.129 --> 00:03:01.700
the light from a point is focussed on the
retina only!

00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:06.680
Yes, it just fine tunes all
the light so that the image is formed on the

00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:09.250
retina and not anywhere else.

00:03:09.250 --> 00:03:11.630
But
how does the lens do that?

00:03:11.630 --> 00:03:16.709
We have studied the properties of images formed
by biconvex lens in the past.

00:03:16.709 --> 00:03:20.459
We know that for different positions of an
object, the image is formed at

00:03:20.459 --> 00:03:21.980
different locations.

00:03:21.980 --> 00:03:26.150
But this does not happen inside the normal
eye.

00:03:26.150 --> 00:03:29.620
The lens
inside the eye always forms the image on the

00:03:29.620 --> 00:03:31.200
retina only!

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:33.790
Not in front or
behind the retina!

00:03:33.790 --> 00:03:35.110
Why is that?

00:03:35.110 --> 00:03:39.459
This is because the size and the shape of
this lens are NOT fixed.

00:03:39.459 --> 00:03:42.310
This is a
flexible lens composed of fibrous jelly like

00:03:42.310 --> 00:03:43.310
material.

00:03:43.310 --> 00:03:48.739
Therefore the lens can
temporarily change its shape up to a certain

00:03:48.739 --> 00:03:49.739
extent.

00:03:49.739 --> 00:03:50.739
But how?

00:03:50.739 --> 00:03:55.790
This movement of the lens is controlled by
muscles called CILIARY MUSCLES.

00:03:55.790 --> 00:03:59.970
One of the most widely accepted theories says
that when the ciliary muscles

00:03:59.970 --> 00:04:04.950
are in a RELAXED position, the lens is THINNER
and hence it has a LARGER

00:04:04.950 --> 00:04:06.930
FOCAL LENGTH.

00:04:06.930 --> 00:04:10.430
And when these muscles contract, thickness
increases and

00:04:10.430 --> 00:04:13.350
hence the focal length decreases.

00:04:13.350 --> 00:04:18.180
So when the object is nearer, the lens increases
its thickness to form its image

00:04:18.180 --> 00:04:19.829
on the retina.

00:04:19.829 --> 00:04:23.279
For a faraway object it becomes thin and the
image is again

00:04:23.279 --> 00:04:24.800
formed on the retina.

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:27.280
So that’s the whole point.

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:29.069
By adjusting its shape, the lens

00:04:29.069 --> 00:04:34.050
makes sure that the image is always formed
on the RETINA only!

00:04:34.050 --> 00:04:35.749
Not in front
or behind the retina!

00:04:35.749 --> 00:04:36.749
Ciliary muscles relaxed  Lens Thinner  Larger
focal length

00:04:36.749 --> 00:04:37.749
Ciliary muscles contract  Lens Thicker
 Smaller focal length

00:04:37.749 --> 00:04:42.619
The ability of the lens of the eye to adjust
its focal length accordingly is called

00:04:42.619 --> 00:04:43.650
ACCOMMODATION.

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:47.629
Now obviously there is some limit to which
the lens can increase or decrease

00:04:47.629 --> 00:04:49.509
its focal length.

00:04:49.509 --> 00:04:52.740
One of the cases is when the object is too
close to the normal

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:54.120
eye…

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:57.460
If you take an object very close to your eye,
the image appears to be

00:04:57.460 --> 00:04:58.460
distorted.

00:04:58.460 --> 00:05:02.349
You will not be able to see the object clearly.

00:05:02.349 --> 00:05:05.909
This is because the
image is not formed on the retina.

00:05:05.909 --> 00:05:11.669
In that case we have to strain our eyes in
order to view the object with clarity.

00:05:11.669 --> 00:05:15.930
When we strain our eyes, what we’re
actually doing is trying to reduce the FOCAL

00:05:15.930 --> 00:05:17.169
LENGTH of the lens.

00:05:17.169 --> 00:05:20.410
We can try
that but we will still not be able to see

00:05:20.410 --> 00:05:22.729
the object clearly!

00:05:22.729 --> 00:05:24.909
So how should we fix that problem?

00:05:24.909 --> 00:05:26.710
The solution is simple.

00:05:26.710 --> 00:05:28.930
The object should be kept at such a distance
from the

00:05:28.930 --> 00:05:33.050
eye that the image is formed on the retina
only.

00:05:33.050 --> 00:05:37.219
This distance is called
MINIMUM DISTANCE of the object from the eye…

00:05:37.219 --> 00:05:42.319
and for the normal eye its
value is around twenty five centimetres.

00:05:42.319 --> 00:05:45.719
Ok, so what did we learn from this video?

00:05:45.719 --> 00:05:49.589
We learned how the light propagates inside
the eye.

00:05:49.589 --> 00:05:52.800
Most of the light is
refracted at the air-cornea interface and

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:56.860
then falls on the lens after travelling
through aqueous humor.

00:05:56.860 --> 00:05:59.309
The lens then focuses all the light THROUGH
the

00:05:59.309 --> 00:06:03.999
vitreous humor, ON the retina by adjusting
its focal length.

00:06:03.999 --> 00:06:07.909
In next video, we will study a few eye defects.

